@page "/toast"

<CatHead Title="Toasts" Tier="@CatHead.Tier3">
    A confirmation that appears, says what happened, and goes away. Anything the reader still has to
    act on is an <a href="/alert">alert</a>, which stays until the state changes.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Toast.MoreTones" Title="Brand and secret" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>Beside go, warn, danger and info, a toast takes <code>brand</code> and <code>secret</code>, from script as <code>kind</code> too.</p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Toast.ToastTriggers" Title="Raising one">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> <code>sednaUi.toast(message, options)</code> renders the stack and the
        toast, stacks a second under the first, and removes it after its timeout;
        <code>timeout: 0</code> keeps it until dismissed, which is what a failure wants. From C# it
        is <code>ToastAsync</code>. <code>kind</code> is <code>go</code>, <code>warn</code>,
        <code>danger</code> or <code>info</code>, and <code>info</code> is the default.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.ToastHandleFromCsharp" Title="Work that ends later" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live — upload it.</strong> <code>ToastAsync</code> returns a
        <code>SednaToast</code>: show <em>Uploading…</em> with <code>timeoutMs: 0</code>, and when the
        work ends <code>ReplaceAsync</code> the outcome into the same toast, in place.
        <code>DismissAsync</code> removes it early. A toast the reader already closed is not an error:
        replacing it shows a new one, because the outcome still has to reach them. The close button's
        name is <code>SednaUiOptions.ToastDismissLabel</code> — the one word the library writes
        itself — or <code>dismissLabel</code> for one toast.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<h2 id="yours">A toast you write <CatAnchor Id="yours" /></h2>
<p class="cat-lede">
    A notification that arrives while the reader is elsewhere &mdash; a task assigned, a value to
    check &mdash; is a toast with something to author: a body that opens the record, buttons of its
    own. Write it once in a <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> and present it in the library's stack, so it
    has the top layer above an open modal, the hold on its timer and one corner shared with every
    other toast. Never position a second stack of your own: it collides with this one and loses all
    three.
</p>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Toast.NotificationCard" Title="A notification card" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> <code>sednaUi.toast.present(templateId, data, options)</code> clones
        the template's first element into the stack and fills its <code>data-*</code> slots with text
        from <code>data</code> &mdash; <code>title</code> fills <code>[data-title]</code> &mdash; and
        <code>href</code> becomes the <code>href</code> of its <code>[data-href]</code> link. A body
        that is the thing to click is an <code>a.toast-body</code> or a
        <code>button.toast-body</code>, so <kbd>Tab</kbd> reaches it apart from the close button;
        <code>.toast-actions</code> holds buttons under the text. A control with
        <code>data-toast-value</code> ends the toast with that value, which
        <code>sednaUi.toast.result(id)</code> resolves with; a <code>.toast-close</code> ends it with
        <code>null</code>, as a dismissal or the timeout do. A link still navigates.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.NotificationsFromCsharp" Title="Notifications from C#, with +N more" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; press it five times.</strong> <code>PresentToastAsync</code> returns a
        <code>SednaToast</code>, and <code>ResultAsync</code> waits for what the reader did with it
        &mdash; <code>"open"</code>, <code>"read"</code>, or <code>null</code> &mdash; for as long as
        that takes, the way <code>ShowModalAsync</code> waits for a dialog. Three cards show at once;
        the rest fold into one card whose count <code>ReplaceFromTemplateAsync</code> moves in place,
        keeping its position and the wait already on it. A template that is not there answers
        <code>null</code> rather than throwing into the circuit.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Toast.Toast" Title="The markup" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        You do not write this for <code>sednaUi.toast</code>, and a toast you write goes in a
        <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> for <code>toast.present</code>. It is here for an app that
        renders a stack inside its own page, such as a server-rendered one. <strong>The stack carries the live region, and on this path it is yours to
        write:</strong> <code>role="status"</code> plus <code>aria-live="polite"</code> on
        <code>.toast-stack</code>, or a toast an app rendered is never announced at all. Do not put
        <code>role="alert"</code> on an individual toast — two live regions announce the same text
        twice, and a failure that has to interrupt gets <code>aria-live="assertive"</code> on the
        stack while it is showing.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Toast.ToastOrAlert" Title="Toast or alert" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The same event, told both ways. A toast is a receipt for something that already happened and
        needs nothing from the reader; an <a href="/alert">alert</a> stays until the state changes,
        and is where an action the reader <em>must</em> take belongs. A notification card can offer
        one, but a toast can be dismissed, so nothing that has to happen may depend on it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatSnippet Name="Toast/ToastApi.txt" Title="The API">
    <p>
        The JavaScript call returns a function that removes that toast early, whose
        <code>id</code> also names it for <code>toast.dismiss(id)</code> and
        <code>toast.replace(id, message, options)</code>. <code>toast.present</code> and
        <code>toast.result</code> are a toast you write and how it ended. <code>dismissLabel</code> names the close
        button, and <code>configure({ toastDismissLabel })</code> sets it once for every toast.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>
